module: stop forcing the ignore-vermagic and ignore-modversions flags

load_module() ORed MODULE_INIT_IGNORE_MODVERSIONS and
MODULE_INIT_IGNORE_VERMAGIC into the caller's flags (a "FIXME" of the
vendor tree, there for prebuilt modules from another build). With
CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_FORCE=y and CONFIG_MODULE_FORCE_LOAD off that refused
every module, in two steps:

- module_sig_check() verifies the signature only when flags == 0 (a
  module with its version information removed is no longer the module
  that was signed), so with the forced flags it never verified, its
  initial -ENOKEY stood and modprobe said "Required key not available",
  silently, even for a valid signature made by the kernel's own key;
- check_modinfo() turns the vermagic check into try_to_force_load()
  when IGNORE_VERMAGIC is set, and that returns -ENOEXEC unless
  CONFIG_MODULE_FORCE_LOAD is on ("Exec format error").

Every module of this kernel is built with it, so vermagic and the
symbol versions match; the checks are the stock ones again.

--- a/kernel/module.c
+++ b/kernel/module.c
@@ -3759,10 +3759,6 @@
 	long err;
 	char *after_dashes;
 
-	//FIXME
-	flags |= MODULE_INIT_IGNORE_MODVERSIONS;
-	flags |= MODULE_INIT_IGNORE_VERMAGIC;
-
 	err = module_sig_check(info, flags);
 	if (err)
 		goto free_copy;
